<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Transylvania</h1><p class="st-meta">1570 CE – 1711 CE · capital Gyulafehérvár</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Semi-independent Ottoman vassal state in the Carpathians, famed for its religious tolerance and for princes who played the Habsburgs and Ottomans against each other.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The principality grew out of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eastern-hungarian-kingdom">Eastern Hungarian Kingdom</a>. By the Treaty of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-speyer">Speyer</a> in 1570, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-sigismund-zapolya">John Sigismund Zápolya</a> renounced the Hungarian royal title and became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-transylvania">Prince of Transylvania</a>, ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partium">Partium</a>, eastern Hungarian counties, under Ottoman suzerainty with his court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alba-iulia">Gyulafehérvár</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Princes were elected by a diet of the three privileged nations, the Hungarian nobles, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saxons">Saxons</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szekelys">Székelys</a>, and paid tribute to the sultan. Four religions were received: Catholic, Lutheran, Calvinist and Unitarian, while the Romanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> majority had no political rights. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-bathory">Stephen Báthory</a> became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1576, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabriel-bethlen">Gabriel Bethlen</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alba-iulia">Gyulafehérvár</a> a centre of Protestant learning.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-ii-rakoczi">George II Rákóczi's</a> disastrous invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1657 brought Ottoman reprisals. After the Ottoman defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1683, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> accepted <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-habsburg">Habsburg</a> rule through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopoldine-diploma">Leopoldine Diploma</a> of 1691. The failure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii-rakoczi">Francis II Rákóczi's</a> revolt ended at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-szatmar">Peace of Szatmár</a> in 1711, leading to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-principality-of-transylvania">Grand Principality of Transylvania</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1570 CE – 1711 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Balkans and Hungary</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:90.95%;width:2.81%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1570 CE – 1711 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gyulafehérvár</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Stephen Báthory, Gabriel Bethlen, George I Rákóczi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Calvinist, Catholic, Lutheran and Unitarian Christianity; Orthodox</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hungarian, German, Romanian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>, <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Transylvania, Transylvanian Principality</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1640&amp;focus=principality-of-transylvania" aria-label="Principality of Transylvania on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5286 571 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Transylvania lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5445 769h0M5709 970h0M5381 747h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5606 827h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5606 827h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Romania. <a href="/atlas?year=1640&amp;focus=principality-of-transylvania">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
